<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Brigantes</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 71 CE (approx.) · capital Isurium Brigantum (Aldborough)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest British confederation, ruling much of northern England under Queen Cartimandua until Rome conquered it after a civil war in the 70s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Brigantes were a grouping of many communities across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-mersey">Mersey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">Tyne</a>. Their name is linked to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigantia">Brigantia</a>. By the late 40s they were ruled by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a>, who had made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and governed as a client ruler.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The confederation was loosely held, combining upland pastoralists and lowland farmers, and had no coinage of its own. Its political centre may have been at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanwick-iron-age-fortifications">Stanwick</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, where vast earthwork enclosures were built in the 1st century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> handed the fugitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 51, and later her divorce from her consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a> split the Brigantes into rival factions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 69 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venutius">Venutius</a> drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> out, and Roman troops could only rescue her. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Petillius Cerialis</a> then conquered the Brigantes from 71, with the legion based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. The southern territory became a civitas centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isurium-brigantum">Isurium Brigantum</a>, Aldborough, in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/brigantes.jpg" alt="Brigantes" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: self-created; derivative work by Jpb1301 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Brigantes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 71 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Isurium Brigantum (Aldborough)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cartimandua, Venutius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Cartimandua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=10&amp;focus=brigantes" aria-label="Brigantes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4634 306 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Brigantes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0M4935 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4954 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4954 562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=10&amp;focus=brigantes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
